.TH ATLAS-TCPDUMP 1 "2026-11-23" "CyberSci Command Atlas" "Command Atlas"
.SH NAME
atlas\-tcpdump \- Capture and print network packets from the command line, or save them for Wireshark.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
sudo tcpdump  \-i lo  \-n  \-nn  \-c 10  \-w cap.pcap  'tcp port 8080 and host 127.0.0.1'
                |      |    |       |         |           '\-\-\- BPF filter (quote it)
                |      |    |       |         '\-\-\- write packets to a file for Wireshark
                |      |    |       '\-\-\- stop after 10 packets
                |      |    '\-\-\- no name or port\-name resolution
                |      '\-\-\- interface (`\-i any` for all)
                '\-\-\- needs root to capture
.fi
.SH WHEN TO USE
.IP \(bu 2
See whether traffic really leaves or reaches a machine.
.IP \(bu 2
Record a capture of an attack on your defence\-challenge service.
.IP \(bu 2
Quick filters when Wireshark is not available.
.SH FLAGS
.TP
\fB\-i IFACE\fR
Interface to listen on.
.br
Use it when: \fBlo\fR for local lab traffic, \fBeth0\fR, \fBany\fR.
.TP
\fB\-n / \-nn\fR
No DNS / no port names.
.br
Use it when: Faster, shows numbers.
.TP
\fB\-c N\fR
Stop after N packets.
.br
Use it when: Short captures.
.TP
\fB\-w FILE / \-r FILE\fR
Write / read a capture file.
.br
Use it when: Hand off to Wireshark.
.TP
\fB\-A / \-X\fR
Show payload as ASCII / hex+ASCII.
.br
Use it when: Read cleartext protocols.
.TP
\fB\-v / \-vv\fR
More protocol detail.
.br
Use it when: Check TTL, flags.
.TP
\fBhost / port / net / tcp / udp\fR
BPF filter words.
.br
Use it when: Narrow what you capture.
.TP
\fBand / or / not\fR
Combine filter words.
.br
Use it when: \fBtcp and not port 22\fR.
.SH RECIPES
.SS "1. Watch local web traffic"
Capture on the loopback interface while making one request.
.PP
.nf
$ sudo tcpdump \-i lo \-nn \-c 6 'tcp port 8099' & sleep 1; curl \-s http://127.0.0.1:8099/ > /dev/null; wait
.fi
.PP
.nf
(expected, not captured) Lines like: IP 127.0.0.1.PORT > 127.0.0.1.8099: Flags [S], seq ... showing the SYN, SYN\-ACK, ACK handshake and the HTTP request.
.fi
.PP
\fB[S]\fR is SYN, \fB[S.]\fR SYN\-ACK, \fB[.]\fR ACK, \fB[P.]\fR data push, \fB[F.]\fR finish. Seeing the handshake proves a connection happened.
.SS "2. See the payload in ASCII"
\fB\-A\fR prints the payload as ASCII: cleartext HTTP, FTP and Telnet credentials are visible.
.PP
.nf
$ sudo tcpdump \-i lo \-nn \-A \-c 8 'tcp port 8099' & sleep 1; curl \-s http://127.0.0.1:8099/hello > /dev/null; wait
.fi
.PP
.nf
(expected, not captured) The packet containing 'GET /hello HTTP/1.1' and headers printed as readable text.
.fi
.PP
This is why encryption matters: on the wire everything is readable.
.SS "3. Save a capture for Wireshark"
\fB\-w\fR stores raw packets; \fB\-r\fR reads them back.
.PP
.nf
$ sudo tcpdump \-i lo \-nn \-c 20 \-w cap.pcap 'tcp port 8099' & sleep 1; curl \-s http://127.0.0.1:8099/ > /dev/null; wait; tcpdump \-nn \-r cap.pcap | head \-5
.fi
.PP
.nf
(expected, not captured) A few packet lines read back from cap.pcap.
.fi
.PP
Open \fBcap.pcap\fR in Wireshark and use Follow TCP Stream to read the conversation.
.SH COMMON MISTAKES
.IP \(bu 2
Needs root (\fBsudo\fR) or the capture capability.
.IP \(bu 2
Quote the filter so the shell does not eat \fB&\fR or parentheses.
.IP \(bu 2
Capturing on \fBany\fR hides which interface a packet used.
.IP \(bu 2
Only capture traffic you are allowed to see.
.SH PRACTICE
.IP 1. 3
Capture a DNS lookup (\fBudp port 53\fR) and read the query and answer.
.IP 2. 3
Capture an SSH login and find the packet sizes.
.IP 3. 3
Filter for one host and one port.
.SH SEE ALSO
wireshark / tshark, nmap, ss, nc, ngrep.
